{
  "requirement": "Validation MUST report a requirement or example with a missing closing fence or an empty body. Full names of `{req}` and `{example}` definitions MUST be unique across requirements, examples, and named definitions. Habeas MUST report every duplicate full name, including collisions involving named definitions, and identify both the duplicate declaration and the first declaration.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is implemented and tested. One test is not linked to it (see Test coverage).\n\n**Implementation**\n- `collect_definitions` in `src/evidence.rs` is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.ci.definition_errors`. `load_definitions` is also marked, and it turns the collected errors into an `InvalidData` error.\n- **Missing closing fence:** when `!block.closed`, it reports `\"{source}: {noun} {name} is missing a closing colon fence\"`, with the file and line. This applies to `req`, `example` and `definition` blocks.\n- **Empty body:** after option lines are stripped, the body is trimmed. If nothing is left it reports `\"{noun} {name} has no text\"`.\n- **Duplicates:** all declaration kinds are inserted into one `BTreeMap` keyed by full name. `Entry::Occupied` therefore catches a collision between any two of requirement, example and named definition (`DefinitionKind::Term`, written `{definition}`). It reports `\"{source}: duplicate {noun} {name}; first declared at {previous.source}\"`. That names the duplicate declaration through the `file:line` prefix and the first declaration explicitly. Every duplicate is reported, because the loop continues without stopping.\n- **Other checks in the same function:** invalid names, unsupported options and the example-must-cite-a-requirement rule are not part of this requirement. Each is covered by another `{impl}` marker on `collect_definitions`: `ci.identifiers`, `references.anchors` and `examples.definition`. The term-declaration check is covered by `definitions.declaration`, which is cited on `collect_definitions` and `DefinitionKind::noun`. I found no unmarked behaviour.\n\n**Test coverage**\n- `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence` checks the exact missing-fence message with file and line.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` checks a duplicate requirement (`duplicate requirement requirements.one; first declared at md/requirements.md:3`) and an empty requirement body.\n- `example_errors_include_duplicates_invalid_bodies_and_missing_tests` checks:\n  - a requirement/requirement collision,\n  - an example/requirement collision,\n  - an example/example collision,\n  - an empty example body,\n  - an unclosed example.\n- `discovery_errors_fail_before_configuration_or_inference` (in `tests/authoring_batch.rs`) checks that a missing closing fence fails the `author` CLI command with that diagnostic.\n- `tests/definitions.rs::invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported` covers the case involving a named definition. A `{req} x` followed by a `{definition} x` yields `duplicate definition bad.x`. An unclosed `{definition}` is also reported. That test is marked only for `definitions.declaration` and `definitions.references`, not for this requirement. This is a minor traceability gap, but the behaviour is tested and the code path is the same generic duplicate branch.",
